{
  "id": "code-encode-and-decode-strings",
  "title": "Encode and Decode Strings",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "topics": ["string", "design"],
  "asked": "common",
  "statement": "You need to send a list of strings over a channel that only carries a single string. Implement the `Codec` class:\n- `encode(strs)` turns a list of strings into one string.\n- `decode(s)` takes a string produced by `encode` and returns the original list of strings exactly.\n\nThe strings can contain any characters at all: digits, commas, `#`, newlines, non-English letters, or nothing (an empty string), so you can't pick a separator and assume it never appears in the data. An empty list and a list holding one empty string are different inputs and must come back differently. Each test encodes with one `Codec` object and decodes with a separate one, so the encoded string has to carry everything on its own.\n\nConstraints: 0 to 200 strings; each string has 0 to 3,000 characters, and may contain any Unicode characters.",
  "kind": "roundtrip",
  "functionName": "Codec",
  "signature": "class Codec:\n    def encode(self, strs: List[str]) -> str:\n    def decode(self, s: str) -> List[str]:",
  "starterCode": "class Codec:\n    def encode(self, strs: List[str]) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Encode a list of strings to a single string.\"\"\"\n        pass\n\n    def decode(self, s: str) -> List[str]:\n        \"\"\"Decode a string made by encode back to the original list.\"\"\"\n        pass\n",
  "examples": [
    {"input": "strs = [\"interview\", \"prep\"]", "output": "[\"interview\", \"prep\"]", "explanation": "decode(encode(strs)) must return the same list."},
    {"input": "strs = []", "output": "[]", "explanation": "An empty list must survive the round trip as an empty list."},
    {"input": "strs = [\"\", \"a#b\", \"\"]", "output": "[\"\", \"a#b\", \"\"]", "explanation": "Empty strings and a # inside a string must come back unchanged."}
  ],
  "visibleTestCount": 3,
