    {"args": [["1#a1#b", "2#"]], "expected": ["1#a1#b", "2#"]}
  ],
  "compare": "exact",
  "referenceSolution": "class Codec:\n    def encode(self, strs: List[str]) -> str:\n        return \"\".join(f\"{len(s)}#{s}\" for s in strs)\n\n    def decode(self, s: str) -> List[str]:\n        strs = []\n        i = 0\n        while i < len(s):\n            j = s.index(\"#\", i)\n            size = int(s[i:j])\n            strs.append(s[j + 1:j + 1 + size])\n            i = j + 1 + size\n        return strs\n",
  "targetComplexity": {"time": "O(total characters)", "space": "O(total characters)"},
  "hints": [
    "Joining the strings with a comma fails for [\"a,b\"]. Whatever separator you choose, what input defeats it?",
    "Instead of hunting for a character that never appears in the data, could the encoded string tell the decoder how long each piece is?",
    "If each string were written with its length in front of it, what would the decoder do to read the first piece? And the next one?",
    "The length has to be separable from the string's own content. How does the decoder know where the length ends, even if the string itself starts with digits?",
    "Write len(s), then a # marker, then s for every string and concatenate. To decode, read up to the next # from the current position to get the length, slice that many characters, and continue after them."
  ],
  "followUps": [
    "What are the time and space complexity of encode and decode?",
    "A string like \"3#abc\" contains your marker and digits. Why does your decoder still read it correctly?",
    "How does your format tell an empty list apart from a list holding one empty string?",
    "An alternative is to escape the separator inside each string. How does that compare, and what edge cases appear?"
  ],
  "idealAnswerPoints": [
    "Prefixes each string with its length so content can never be confused with structure",
